<!-- Algorithms In-Depth Breakdown -->
<div class="card shadow-xs" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; padding: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Core Algorithms & Execution Mechanics</h3>
    <p class="text-muted" style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem; font-size: 0.88rem;">
        Transparent, auditable algorithmic designs power each layer of the CodeGuard analysis pipeline.
    </p>

    <div class="two-col-layout">
        <div class="p-3" style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <div class="font-bold text-primary" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">1. AST Import Visitor & Graph Assembly</div>
            <p class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                Parses raw Python code into an Abstract Syntax Tree via <code>ast.parse()</code>. An <code>ast.NodeVisitor</code> inspects <code>Import</code> and <code>ImportFrom</code> nodes to resolve exact module paths, constructing directed dependency edges <code>(A &rarr; B)</code>.
            </p>
            <div style="font-family: ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 0.78rem; background: #0f172a; color: #38bdf8; padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 4px;">
                def visit_ImportFrom(self, node):<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;module = self._resolve(node.module)<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;self.graph.add_edge(current_file, module)
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="p-3" style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <div class="font-bold text-primary" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">2. BFS Blast Radius Propagation</div>
            <p class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                When a commit modifies file <code>F</code>, Breadth-First Search traverses inverted dependency edges to collect direct dependents (Depth 1) and transitive dependents (Depth 2+), bounding the blast radius.
            </p>
            <div style="font-family: ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 0.78rem; background: #0f172a; color: #34d399; padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 4px;">
                queue = deque([(F, 0)])<br>
                while queue and depth &lt; MAX_DEPTH:<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;curr, depth = queue.popleft()<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;for dep in reverse_graph[curr]: queue.append((dep, depth + 1))
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Interactive FAQ & SCM Glossary Accordion -->
<div class="card shadow-xs" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; padding: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Frequently Asked Questions & SCM Glossary</h3>
    <p class="text-muted" style="margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 0.88rem;">
        Clear answers to common questions about SCM baseline compliance, test optimization, and risk evaluation.
    </p>

    <div class="faq-accordion">
        <div class="faq-item">
            <div class="faq-question" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>What is the difference between a Git commit and an SCM baseline?</span>
                <span class="faq-icon">&#9662;</span>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-answer">
                A Git commit is an individual point-in-time snapshot of repository files. An SCM baseline (per IEEE 828) is a formally reviewed, verified, and agreed-upon version of a software configuration item that serves as the basis for further development and release candidate auditing.
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <div class="faq-question" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>How does Regression Test Selection (RTS) prevent test escapes?</span>
                <span class="faq-icon">&#9662;</span>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-answer">
                CodeGuard's AST analyzer maps exact syntactic call and import graphs. Any test suite that imports a modified file—or imports a file that depends on the modified file—is automatically selected for execution. By maintaining a 100% downstream coverage rule, redundant tests are skipped while zero affected paths escape verification.
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <div class="faq-question" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>How is the risk score formula calibrated?</span>
                <span class="faq-icon">&#9662;</span>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-answer">
                The formula balances three pillars of risk: <code>(Dependents × 2) + (Defects × 3) + (Lines ÷ 20)</code>.
                Downstream dependents evaluate systemic architectural exposure, past defect files capture code instability/fragility, and lines changed gauge pure review complexity. Scores &lt; 5 are Low Risk, 5–15 Medium Risk, and &gt; 15 High Risk.
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <div class="faq-question" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>Can CodeGuard run against custom Git repositories?</span>
                <span class="faq-icon">&#9662;</span>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-answer">
                Yes! CodeGuard leverages GitPython to dynamically inspect any local repository worktree or clone. By executing <code>git log</code>, <code>git diff</code>, and <code>git blame</code> natively, it functions autonomously across standard Git workflows.
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<script>
function filterConcepts(query) {
    const q = query.toLowerCase().trim();
    document.querySelectorAll('.academic-card').forEach(card => {
        const text = card.textContent.toLowerCase();
        card.style.display = (!q || text.includes(q)) ? '' : 'none';
    });
}

function toggleFaq(el) {
    const item = el.closest('.faq-item');
    item.classList.toggle('open');
}
</script>

